<!-- Website-only teaching for android/performance/startup.md; original chapter remains read-only. Sources checked 2026-10-25. -->

<!-- @opening -->

<p class="story-lead">You tap the app icon. A spinner appears, then you wait for the feed. The app has shown something, but it has not yet delivered what you came for. Optimising that launch starts with choosing which finish line matters.</p>

Read this as a critical-path investigation: what must finish before the first useful screen, which work blocks it, and which experiment would prove an improvement.

<!-- @after The Cold-Start Pipeline :: visual -->

### Two finish lines, one user journey

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-flow" style="--cols:3"><div class="node"><strong>Launch requested</strong><small>Start from a specified entry point and launch state.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Initial display · TTID</strong><small>The first app frame is visible; a loading UI can satisfy this boundary.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Useful content · TTFD</strong><small>The app reports fully drawn when the chosen screen is ready to use.</small></div></div><figcaption>A fast first frame and fast useful content are separate goals. State what “ready” means for this screen before collecting numbers.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Macrobenchmark — Measuring Startup Properly :: animation -->

### Find the work that delays useful content

<figure class="stepper"><span class="stepper-title">Find the work that delays useful content</span><div class="stage viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node" data-el="launch"><strong>Launch</strong><small>Cold-start experiment</small></div><div class="node" data-el="main"><strong>Main thread</strong><small>Initialisation and UI</small></div><div class="node" data-el="ready"><strong>Ready boundary</strong><small>Feed content visible</small></div><div class="node" data-el="proof"><strong>Evidence</strong><small>Trace plus repeated timings</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[{"label": "Choose a journey", "caption": "Launch the same entry point with a defined process, data and compilation state. This is a conceptual experiment, not a recorded app trace.", "state": {"launch": "active"}, "add": ["launch", "main", "ready", "proof"]}, {"label": "Locate the first display", "caption": "Inspect startup work before TTID. A placeholder finishing early does not establish that the feed is usable.", "state": {"launch": "done", "main": "active"}}, {"label": "Follow the dependency", "caption": "Continue to the app-defined ready condition and reportFullyDrawn boundary. Identify the dependency that delays that condition.", "state": {"main": "done", "ready": "active"}}, {"label": "Change one dependency", "caption": "Remove or defer only work proven unnecessary for that boundary, respecting the API’s threading contract. Repeat the same journey and retain the trace.", "state": {"ready": "done", "proof": "active"}}]</script><figcaption>Illustrative sequence. Use Previous, Next or Play; every step is also available as text.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Diagnosing Slow Startup :: staff -->

Keep launch mode, entry point, device tier and content state beside the metric. A cohort regression needs its own trace; fleet averages cannot identify a blocked dependency.

<!-- @before Further Reading :: check -->

### Check your diagnosis

<details><summary>The spinner appears sooner, but the feed is ready at the same time. What improved?</summary>

TTID may have improved. The useful-content boundary did not; report both rather than calling the whole launch faster.

</details>

<details><summary>Why record compilation state with a cold-start result?</summary>

Cold describes process state. Already compiled code, installed profiles and app data can still differ between runs.

</details>

<details><summary>Which first screen should define readiness for a deep link?</summary>

The destination the user requested. A launcher feed benchmark alone does not validate every entry point.

</details>

<!-- @before Further Reading :: deeper -->

Primary evidence for the added layer: [Startup boundaries](https://developer.android.com/topic/performance/issues/launch-time), [Startup measurement](https://developer.android.com/topic/performance/benchmarking/macrobenchmark-metrics).

